    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, I'm studying for my ground school exam and I'm a bit confused about lift. Can you explain how Bernoulli's principle and Newton's third law actually work together to create lift on a wing? I want to make sure I understand the physics behind it, not just the definitions.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Bernoulli's principle and Newton's third law combine to create lift on a wing when air flows over and under the curvedupper surface. As the air speeds up over the curved surface, its pressure decreases due to Bernoulli's principle. Conversely, the air flows slower and has more pressure below the wing, creating an upward force on the wing. This difference in pressure generates the lift that opposes the weight of the aircraft.",
      "source": "target",
